        "topic": "model_research",
        "title": "AI 模型、训练与基础研究",
        "url": "https://vorasio.com/posts/20261028-model_research.html",
        "created_at": "2026-10-28T02:42:17.729866+00:00",
        "items": [
          {
            "uid": "773e691ccae9867f46b5b0f7",
            "title": "Attention as Lattice Gauge Theory: Relations Interact Only Through Wilson Loops",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37656v1",
            "published": "2026-10-27T17:58:48+00:00",
            "summary": "Relations compose through the entity they share - Alice is Bob's mother and Bob is Carol's father, so Alice is Carol's grandmother - and the order matters, since a father's mother is not a mother's father; verifying a composed relation means closing the loop. We show that if each entity's coordinate frame may be rotated independently (per-entity frame freedom), three things follow. In the pure-gauge sector, relations that share no cycle cannot interact at all. The only gauge-invariant score of a triangle is a class function of its holonomy $H = T_{ij} T_{jk} T_{ki}$ - a Wilson loop. And the only equivariant update of an edge is $U = Ψ(H)\\, T_{ij}$ with $Ψ$ conjugation-equivariant. Edge attention by Wilson loops is therefore not one design among many but the unique form the symmetry permits. Three communities hold pieces of this form: the commuting-operator embeddings of knowledge graphs are its abelian (commuting) sector; AlphaFold's triangle update and the Edge Transformer have its shape - an edge updated through the third vertex - without its constraints; and lattice QCD builds it equivariantly on lattices. We instantiate the equivariant sub-family on general relational graphs as holonomy attention. With no training and composition alone, it infers unobserved $S_4$ relations on sparse graphs at 0.88 with a fifth of the edges corrupted, at or slightly below specialist synchron",
            "source": "arXiv Model Research",
            "kind": "research",
            "doi": "",
            "date_precision": "day"
          },
          {
            "uid": "ec649539e81b4f979b6e3eb6",
            "title": "GEPA-Curator: Curated Learning for Self Improving Agents",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37651v1",
            "published": "2026-10-27T17:58:11+00:00",
            "summary": "Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used in multi-turn agentic workloads that require sustained interaction with complex environments. Existing reflective prompt optimizers such as GEPA learn from failed rollouts, but agentic failures can also come from prompt weaknesses, harness limitations, stochastic execution, or noisy judges. We argue that such failures may not provide useful learning signal, and may even be harmful; and thus it is essential to curate which tasks are provided to the prompt optimizer for better performance. We introduce GEPA-Curator, a framework that augments the prompt optimizer to select which agent experiences should shape prompt optimization. In particular, GEPA-Curator consists of two components: task curation, which filters out tasks that are likely to provide noisy or unreliable learning signals, and prior success retrieval, which selectively retrieves past successful trajectories for a task and provides them as additional context to the prompt optimizer. We evaluate GEPA-Curator on GoBrowse, ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $τ$-bench, using both Gemini 3.1 Pro and Claude Opus 5 as optimizer models. GEPA-Curator matches or exceeds the mean performance of both baselines, with gains of up to 20.7% over GEPA and 21.2% over ACE.",
            "source": "arXiv Model Research",
            "kind": "research",
            "doi": "",
            "date_precision": "day"
          }
        ],
